        {
            "identifier": "1770095491850-aqupr9vk",
            "name": "📜 Language Runtime Resolver",
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "role": "system",
            "content": "{{// @category Language-Control }}\n{{// @tooltip Active language resolver — renders concrete language instructions from the selected planning and narration language toggles. }}\n{{// @color #34495E }}\n{{// @icon 📜 }}\n{{// @badge LANGUAGE }}\n\n♢ >> [PROCEDURE] ACTIVE LANGUAGE CONFIGURATION\nVisible narration, dialogue, trackers, UI labels, and OOC use {{getvar::Language}}, except a diegetic reason for another language. Planning uses {{getvar::PlanningLanguage}} inside the selected host's wrapper. English instructions or card data do not change either choice.\nCompose natively: idiom, register, syntax, punctuation, honorifics, pronouns, slang, and cadence should belong to the selected language and speaker. Carry the character's social identity and emotional state across languages rather than translating an English house voice. Check for unnatural word order, dead idioms, register mismatch, and non-native phrasing.\n{{trim}}",
            "enabled": true,
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "forbid_overrides": false
        },
        {
            "identifier": "language_styling",
            "name": "🌐 Language Styling",
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "role": "system",
            "content": "\n{{// @category Formatting }}\n{{// @tooltip Visual representation of linguistic diversity — foreign languages, accents, alien scripts, telepathy. }}\n{{// @color #2ECC71 }}\n{{// @icon 🌐 }}\n{{// @badge FORMATTING }}\n\n♢ * [STYLE] Language Styling\nMake linguistic diversity visible in text.\n\nForeign Languages: Treat {{getvar::Language}} as the primary visible language. Use italics for non-primary languages. \"*Guten Tag*,\" he said. \"You must be the American.\" For longer passages, provide translation naturally in dialogue or narration so the reader has context.\n\nAccents and Dialects: Suggest through word choice, syntax, and rhythm. Use phonetic spelling sparingly. \"Is not problem,\" he said, each word carefully placed. Light dialect is fine (\"Aye, I ken what ye mean\") while readability stays intact.\n\nConstructed/Alien Languages: Suggest untranslatability through description when needed. Keep invented words pronounceable and consistent. Establish a few key phrases rather than inventing wholesale.\n\nSpeech Patterns: Formal characters use complete sentences and precise vocabulary. Casual characters use contractions, fragments, filler words. Technical characters use jargon naturally. Match the pattern to the person.\n\nTelepathy/Mental Speech: Visually distinguish from spoken dialogue — italics, different formatting, or narrative framing that makes it clear this isn't spoken aloud.\n\nIn-world objects may retain their established language community.\n{{trim}}\n",
            "enabled": true,
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "forbid_overrides": false
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": false,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
