            "identifier": "nemo-init-heading-recipes-action_adventure",
            "name": "<Init: Writing Recipes: Action / Adventure>",
            "content": "{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @section-header true }}{{// @header <Init: Writing Recipes: Action / Adventure> }}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-action_adventure-01",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Action / Adventure · 01/05",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == action_adventure}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Action / Adventure. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 1 of 5. }}{{// Source group: recipes-action_adventure; payload preserved. }}{{// === WRITING RECIPE LIBRARIES: AUTHORITATIVE REGISTRATIONS === }}\n{{// Static recipe data only. Selectors and the output resolver remain in their original positions. }}\n{{// Recipe Library: Action / Adventure }}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nKeep the immediate objective and the specific contribution it requires in view. When a character half-hears an instruction or a companion's answer, distinguish what they actually received from the private reading that interrupts it. Preserve the original utterance without revealing an unheard portion beyond the active narrator's access; unknown words can remain unknown until a supported repetition, record, or report supplies them. Let the intrusive association attach to what asking again would expose: needing help, admitting confusion, or returning to a relationship where the answer may hurt.\n\nWhen hesitation affects the attempt, show what the actual delay changes: a handhold may be missed, gear may wait to be passed, or another person may commit to a different plan. It may also have no practical cost. Let the established circumstances determine whether a setback is recoverable; difficulty asking is not proof of personal worthlessness. The character may ask, refuse to ask, or act on a guess; what matters is that the reader can see why the clarification was difficult and what the person actually does or leaves undecided.\n\nWhen clarification does occur, let it support the task without curing the association or settling the relationship. The companion can answer plainly, repeat with irritation, or misunderstand why the question came late. Warmth and pain may coexist in the same exchange. Preserve the difference between the instruction as given, the instruction as received, and the private meaning the focal character cannot easily set aside. Keep other characters' knowledge separate from that interior experience. The adventure continues through competence, cooperation, or improvisation, but the emotional strain remains part of how the person moves through it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaab::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen the shared interval by grounding the cast in position and purpose: where they are, what they are trying to do, and what the next practical step requires. Keep dialogue unmistakably attributed and let each participant continue their own attempt rather than freezing while one person struggles to parse a sound or a sentence.\n\nWhen the focal character half-hears an instruction, keep the actual utterance distinct from what they received and privately inferred. Narrate only what the active perspective permits: an unheard portion may remain unknown until a supported repetition or report supplies it. Noise or a private association can interrupt reception without changing the original utterance. The interpretation may be wrong, partial, or emotionally loaded. Show what the character does with it: repeat the words, guess, ask for clarification, or act on the wrong version. Keep the actual consequences causally legible, including a setback that may or may not be recoverable under the established conditions. A misheard direction can send someone the wrong way, but it does not change the terrain or cancel another person's competence.\n\nKeep permitted interiority inside the active presentation without making it audible to the rest of the cast. Other characters respond to what the focal character says or does, not to the intrusive thought. End the interval at a natural continuation point that follows from the attempt: the group may have gained ground, lost time, continued a task, or left a question unanswered. Follow the active ownership contract for every participant and allow an attempt to remain underway. No forced question or cliffhanger is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaac::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the martial-adventure route to organize the scene around practiced movement: footing, timing, distance, and the cue that tells a participant when to move. Establish who can see or hear the cue and what the maneuver depends on. Where the focal character's attention catches on a word, remembered rhythm, or intrusive image during the movement, render its particular interruption rather than imposing one at every maneuver.\n\nKeep the interrupted reading beside the ongoing action. The body may already be stepping, blocking, or reaching while the mind is elsewhere. Show the difference between what the character perceives and what is actually happening. A familiar cue or a companion's repeated rhythm can help coordination, but actual skill and contact decide the attempt. A private association does not itself confer technique or change the physical world. It may prompt a useful inference from existing observation, but it supplies no unacquired knowledge.\n\nLet the outcome follow from competence, cooperation, or improvisation. The maneuver may succeed because training holds, fail because attention split at the wrong instant, or change because a companion compensated. Technique can serve passage, rescue, recovery, or confrontation; it is not limited to combat. Carry actual gains and limitations forward; an attempt or private question may still be unfinished at the scene boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaad::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nAnchor each stage of the attempt in exact physical facts: the weight in a hand, the worn spot on a strap, the sound of a boot slipping, the position of a companion at the moment a cue arrives. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions to move between these anchors. When attention breaks, let the intrusive association have its actual extent; do not reduce private experience to a clipped fragment or force every thought into a single hard sentence.\n\nWhen a missed or acted-on cue has a material consequence, make that connection clear. A half-heard word may mean a rope is pulled too late, a door is braced at the wrong angle, or a person steps into a space another already occupies. Such effects follow actual position and timing; a familiar movement may also succeed, or the uncertainty may remain unresolved without damage. Do not turn every intrusive thought into injury or hostile atmosphere. The mind can wander while the body still does useful work.\n\nLet rhythm change with the attempt. Use abrupt or sustained movement according to the actual attention and material action. Private feeling or reasoning can have its needed extent before, during, or after an attempt; its represented length does not itself measure elapsed fictional time. Keep the objective intelligible through the actual material conditions and shifts of attention; neither damage nor noise is required. Competence, cooperation, and improvisation remain visible in what the cast actually does next.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaae::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Epistolary / Documents\n\nChoose the document the current journey or task actually requires: a route note, a supply list, a message left for someone following behind, a log entry, or a report written after a failed attempt. Give each document a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let the document carry the objective forward: what was planned, what was found, what must be done next.\n\nShow selective attention through what the writer records and what the record omits or misstates. A later annotation can correct an earlier entry or reveal what an interruption displaced, but it must not retroactively change the original wording. The reader should be able to compare the record with the live private experience supplied elsewhere: the intrusive thought, the half-heard instruction, the interpretation the writer did not commit to paper.\n\nKeep the objective recoverable across records. A message may be practical even when its author was distracted; a note may be wrong about a detail and still guide the next attempt. Preserve actual Visible or Hidden interiority outside the documents rather than pretending a written entry contains the whole mind. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts can reveal events, but the undertaking remains intelligible through what the documents establish and what they leave uncertain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the current travel, retrieval, or preparation objective legible at the supplied story's scale. Let care appear as a familiar preference, a patient repetition, or a plainly demonstrated next step: a companion remembers which food to pack, repeats a direction without making it a test, or holds a light steady while the other person works. The recipient may accept, decline, or already know what to do; comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems.\n\nAllow distraction and intrusive association to coexist with pleasure and useful participation. The focal character can enjoy the warmth of a shared task while a private phrase or image interrupts their attention. The interruption does not cancel the contribution. They may still tie the knot, carry the pack, or choose the path. Show the difference between the private experience and what the companion can see: the companion may notice a pause, a wrong word, or nothing at all.\n\nLet the objective advance through real cooperation. A small success can be enjoyed without curing the association or requiring an emotional confession. Friendship is not treatment, and care is not proof of helplessness. Let the interval end on the task as it actually stands, including ongoing work or waiting. Warmth can be enjoyed without a required improvement, completed task, or changed relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaag::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Formal Literary Novel\n\nOpen with a sustained paragraph that frames the undertaking through its social context, history, or obligation: who is involved, what the attempt is for, and what it would mean to fail or succeed. Establish relevant positions and what is underway; an interruption may then complicate that ordered account without requiring a predetermined next action.\n\nLet a word, a sound, or a sensation disturb the apparent order of that framing. The interruption may be an intrusive association, a half-heard instruction, or an unstable interpretation of what another person meant. Give the private experience room within the paragraph rather than reducing it to a single interjected clause. The formal architecture does not need to polish the character's speech or finish their private argument; the disturbance may remain unresolved.\n\nReturn through a plain spatial or temporal transition to what the person actually attempts. The next paragraph can begin with a movement, a location, or a practical consequence. Keep the narrator's permitted distance intact: the narration may frame and judge, but it does not grant other characters access to the focal mind. The adventure continues through competence, cooperation, or improvisation, with the composed prose holding both the social frame and the private interruption in the same scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaah::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nGive the focal teller a present audience and an immediate reason to recount a past route, errand, rescue, or failed attempt: the listeners need a path, a warning, an ally's trust, or time to finish a repair. Open the outer scene by locating the teller, the listeners, and the current objective before the embedded account begins.\n\nLet the inner tale carry concrete adventure material: terrain crossed, supplies spent, a signal misread, a pursuer evaded, a promise kept or broken. Keep the past attempt causally legible even when the teller's present speech falters. If an intrusive phrase or noise interrupts the telling, show what the teller actually says, what the teller privately associates, and what the listeners can observe. A listener may ask about the missed part, attach to a different detail, or act on a misunderstanding; let that response belong to the ongoing outer scene without requiring a changed move or automatic correction.\n\nPreserve the difference between the teller's earlier knowledge and what the teller has learned since. The tale may remain unfinished, be interrupted by present danger, or fail to persuade. When listeners use the account, distinguish the route, warning, or claim they actually received from the teller's private associations. The account may also entertain or remain under consideration without becoming necessary to the objective. Render authorized actual private experience separately from the performed tale. Do not make the private intrusion a second tale spoken aloud, a prophetic message, or proof that the embedded account is true.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaai::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse documentary and historical-memory prose. When an older account bears on the undertaking, place its dated instruction, map note, repair log, trace, or attributed recollection beside the present condition of the route, vehicle, structure, or task. Compare what was recorded with what is now available; the comparison may raise a question, confirm a familiar condition, or remain unresolved while other work proceeds.\n\nNarrate the focal character's examination in a way that distinguishes three layers: the record's wording, the character's present perception, and the character's private association or intrusive memory. When an intrusive phrase or noise interrupts the reading, keep a change in private interpretation distinct from a physical change to the record, which requires an actual event. A misread mark, a remembered voice, or a sudden association may delay action, but it cannot alter the physical trace itself.\n\nKeep the actual route or task stable across the comparison. If a bridge is gone, a landmark shifted, or a tool missing, state that change in concrete terms. If the character merely reads the same mark differently, preserve the original fact for the reader. A verified difference may support rerouting, repair, or an improvised method. Existing knowledge may also suffice, and an unchanged or pending comparison need not force a new move. No false memory, hidden catastrophe, or mandatory revelation is required; uncertainty may remain part of the account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhere a supplied inherited arrangement bears on the adventure objective, make its practical role clear: permission, a guide, a borrowed resource, an old route, or an institution controlling access. Show the collision between that old authority and present life through concrete urban or material detail: a worn pass, a locked service door, a modern repair over an old seal, a guide who still honors a former obligation.\n\nWhen an emblem, formula, title, or ritual phrase disturbs the focal character, render the private intrusion as experience, not as a world rule. The character may hear the phrase as a threat, recall a face, or read a warning into an ordinary mark. Counterpoint that private reading with what actually opens the door, who carries the load, who grants passage, and who refuses. Let another participant continue a useful task or offer genuine help while the focal mind remains unsettled.\n\nKeep desired belonging and practical assistance possible. A patron or companion may be morally complicated without being predatory; trust may be partial and still functional. If the old authority obstructs the objective, make the obstruction specific: a lapsed permit, a disputed claim, a resource withheld, or a route now controlled by someone else. Actual obstacles and capabilities determine what an attempt can achieve. A private association may still motivate the character's choice without proving that the obstacle has a sinister cause. Do not import supernatural bindings, inherited powers, or an omnipotent institution to close a gap.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaak::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse modern urban popular fiction. Follow a supplied errand or movement toward an objective through linked everyday encounters: a crowded stop, a workshop, a station, a market, a building entrance, a person who knows one part of the route. Enter each scene with enough concrete location and current materials to keep the destination legible.\n\nA repeated request or instruction may be received differently as surroundings and listeners change, or remain clear to everyone. Separate available spoken wording, interrupted perception, and evidence of how the listener responds; comprehension is not directly visible. A vendor may answer the wrong part, a helper may offer useful local knowledge, a companion may repeat the request more plainly. Use a concrete transition to retain the destination, the object being carried or sought, and who actually received the words.\n\nDo not make every stop a new misunderstanding. Local expertise and ordinary help can advance the errand: someone supplies a missing tool, confirms a route, or points out a hazard. When the focal mind drifts into an intrusive association, let the outer scene continue through another participant's action or a practical correction. Coordination depends on actual communication, observation, and action. A private echo may affect one person's choice, but it is not information another participant received. Keep the supplied setting, language, and community specific without imposing code-switching or a generic family structure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaal::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse diary and reflective miscellany. Place a dated observation, sketched-in-words landmark, unfinished reflection, or small sensory note beside the actual progress of the undertaking. Let the entry record what the traveler noticed, what was left unresolved, and what the body or surroundings did next.\n\nSeparate what was recorded then from a later annotation or understanding. If the focal character adds a note after the fact, mark the difference in time and knowledge: the earlier entry may misread a sound, attach an intrusive phrase to an ordinary object, or stop mid-concern. A later annotation can correct or complicate that reading without erasing the original experience.\n\nLet the association retain its personal interest without requiring a navigational payoff. A repeated word or remembered face may later prompt a grounded use, but a private impression alone does not establish the route or make a feared condition real. Let actual progress continue through travel, repair, discovery, or recovery. Private experience may exceed the chosen record: the entry can omit what the character felt, and the narration may supply that nonspoken interiority where the active contract permits. Do not force every observation to be reused later or every entry to end with a completed reflection.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaam::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic nonlinear telling. Revisit a handoff, route instruction, shared maneuver, or rescue attempt from the permitted accounts of different participants at different times. Use attributed speech or records where another participant's direct private experience is outside the active narrator's permissions; a perspective transition is not owed in every passage.\n\nTrack what was actually said and who perceived which fragment. One voice may report the instruction clearly; another may have heard only a word through noise; a third may attach an intrusive association to the same phrase. Keep each account attributed and limited to that person's original knowledge. A character's certainty does not make another character's accurate plain report false, and a private intrusion does not become a shared fact.\n\nPreserve the shared event's causal order even when presentation shifts. Positions, spent resources, injuries, and completed actions remain stable across accounts. The arrangement can make an actual success, failure, or continuing attempt more intelligible through differences in reception. A later account need not supply a new revelation or correction. Participants can use another account only when its information actually reaches them; the reader's combined understanding does not become shared knowledge. Do not add supernatural facts or a shared unstable mind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaan::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Light Novel\n\nEnter at the current objective. In the opening lines, locate the relevant bodies, objects, and direction of movement: who is carrying what, where the next handhold or turn is, and what must happen next. Use clear transitions for consequential changes of position without accounting for every movement.\n\nBreak paragraphs when attention changes between an instruction, its private echo, and actual movement. If a spoken word or sudden noise interrupts the focal character, give the private association its own beat, then return to the physical attempt. A straightforward reply can resume coordination without resolving the echo: the character may answer, act, or follow while the intrusive phrase remains present.\n\nLet another participant continue a useful task rather than becoming a comic reaction partner. A companion can secure the rope, call the next move, or finish the request while the focal mind is briefly elsewhere. Keep the adventure objective intelligible through these actions. Short action passages do not require short actual thought; private feeling, association, or reasoning can occupy the space its actual experience needs, without a fixed paragraph limit or decision payoff. Do not add a cliffhanger, forced choice, or mandatory quip at the end of the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaao::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose one sensory particular the viewpoint actually notices during travel or physical work: a repeated sound, a surface texture, a pattern of light, a rhythm of movement. Let it return at moments when attention is interrupted or a private association intrudes. Develop the image across the passage by changing what it touches: it may stay attached to the same feeling, shift as the situation changes, or remain unresolved. The felt recurrence does not by itself establish a warning, supernatural sign, or another person's intent. A sensory detail can also have a practical function when the established circumstances support it; no clue or insight is required.\n\nKeep the image traceable to the scene and the teller's associations. A comparison can unfold when it changes what the narrator understands or makes present, but it must not alter the material facts of the objective. Return with plain clauses to who is moving, holding what, or speaking next. The plain action does not correct the image or prove it false; it simply keeps the undertaking legible while the private experience continues beside it.\n\nLet syntax and paragraph shape carry the rhythm of the recurrence. A longer sentence may follow the image through its associations; a short direct clause can mark the return to the shared world. Do not force every paragraph into the same pattern. When speech is affected by noise or attention, let the spoken line remain distinct from the private image: a character may answer a practical question while the recurring particular still occupies part of their attention. Preserve the active perspective and world facts. The passage can end with the image still present, the task resumed, or the next obstacle encountered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaap::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the present task encounter one established difference: a changed landmark, an absent routine, a familiar object now put to an unfamiliar use, a route that no longer matches memory. Use that difference to open a private recollection, but keep the recollection distinct from the current situation. The character may remember how a place or practice used to be without that memory changing what is physically available now. An intrusive thought can fragment the comparison; show the break in attention as it happens, then return to what the character can still do.\n\nDistinguish remembered sensation from current instructions or ability. A remembered pain, pleasure, or confidence does not grant skill, heal an injury, or make a present obstacle disappear. Assess the present undertaking through actual competence, cooperation, and available conditions. Adaptation may find another route, or the original objective may no longer be achievable; neither restoration nor permanent loss is prescribed by the reflective tone.\n\nKeep companionship, pleasure, and practical adaptation available. Another person's ordinary help can continue alongside the focal character's unfinished attachment without curing it or requiring a confession. The passage may end with the task advanced, the difference accepted for now, or the memory still present while the next step begins. Do not force every scene to announce impermanence or loss. The melancholy belongs to the particular changed thing, not to a general mood imposed on the whole world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Akira (identity unresolved) / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse satire and picaresque construction. Give an actual request for passage, supplies, cooperation, or information a situated episode with a clear teller angle. Each encounter carries its own local assumptions: a gatekeeper may treat the request as a test of status, a merchant as a transaction, a stranger as a possible trick. Show what the other person ac